From: MastaG <mastag@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd/display: release the BC-250 GFX override before the commit, not during link training

Releasing the override from inside DP link training is too late. By the time
perform_link_training_with_retries() runs, amdgpu_dm_commit_streams() has
already disabled the old stream and torn the link down -- the PCON has
watched the link go away and come back with the override still latched, and
its HDMI side then fails to come up. Observed directly: with the release
firing at training time, the switch still failed, and on a later attempt the
override was already released for the whole modeset and it failed anyway,
because the damage was done earlier in the commit.

Move the call to the top of amdgpu_dm_atomic_commit_tail(), before
drm_atomic_helper_update_legacy_modeset_state() and before
amdgpu_dm_commit_streams(). That is the earliest point at which the driver
knows a display commit is happening at all, and it covers every modeset,
every DPMS change and every retrain a commit triggers -- rather than the one
sub-phase the previous hook could see.

The link_dp_training.c and link_dpms.c call sites stay. They are cheap
(a no-op once the override is already released) and they re-arm the restore
timer, which is what keeps the release open across the PCON's own HDMI-side
training after the commit returns.

Also adds amdgpu.cs_od_unforce_settle_ms, a pause after the release before
the commit proceeds. The release is itself a clock and voltage transition,
and starting a modeset directly on top of one may be no better than the
override being held. Default 0 -- no pause, no behaviour change -- because it
costs latency on every modeset and has not been shown to be needed; it exists
so the question can be answered without another rebuild.

NOT hardware-tested. This addresses a specific objection to the previous
attempt -- that the release kicks in too late -- and that objection is
well-founded, but whether it is sufficient is exactly what needs testing.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
@@ -109,6 +109,13 @@
 
 static_assert(AMDGPU_DMUB_NOTIFICATION_MAX == DMUB_NOTIFICATION_MAX, "AMDGPU_DMUB_NOTIFICATION_MAX mismatch");
 
+/*
+ * Releases a held GFX clock/voltage override across a display commit. Defined
+ * in drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/cyan_skillfish_ppt.c; a no-op on any
+ * GPU that is not a Cyan Skillfish.
+ */
+extern void cs_od_force_suspend(void);
+
 #define FIRMWARE_RENOIR_DMUB "amdgpu/renoir_dmcub.bin"
 MODULE_FIRMWARE(FIRMWARE_RENOIR_DMUB);
 #define FIRMWARE_SIENNA_CICHLID_DMUB "amdgpu/sienna_cichlid_dmcub.bin"
@@ -11615,6 +11622,21 @@
 
 	trace_amdgpu_dm_atomic_commit_tail_begin(state);
 
+	/*
+	 * BC-250: release a held GFX clock/voltage override before *any*
+	 * display work starts -- before streams are disabled, before the link
+	 * is torn down, before training.
+	 *
+	 * Releasing it from inside link training (link_dp_training.c) proved
+	 * too late: by the time training runs, the PCON has already watched
+	 * the link go away and come back with the override still latched, and
+	 * the HDMI side then fails to come up. This is the earliest point at
+	 * which the driver knows a display commit is happening at all.
+	 *
+	 * Inert on any non-Cyan-Skillfish GPU -- see cs_od_force_suspend().
+	 */
+	cs_od_force_suspend();
+
 	drm_atomic_helper_update_legacy_modeset_state(dev, state);
 	drm_dp_mst_atomic_wait_for_dependencies(state);
 
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/cyan_skillfish_ppt.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/cyan_skillfish_ppt.c
@@ -154,6 +154,19 @@
 		 "BC-250: how long to release a forced GPU clock/voltage override after a display link is brought up, in ms (0 = never release)");
 
 /*
+ * How long to wait after releasing the override before letting the display
+ * commit proceed. The release is a real clock and voltage transition, so
+ * there is an argument for letting it settle rather than starting a modeset
+ * on top of it. Default 0 -- no wait -- because it costs latency on every
+ * modeset and has not been shown to be needed; raise it if a board still
+ * loses its link with the release in place.
+ */
+static uint cs_od_unforce_settle_ms;
+module_param(cs_od_unforce_settle_ms, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(cs_od_unforce_settle_ms,
+		 "BC-250: pause after releasing the GPU clock/voltage override before the display commit proceeds, in ms (0 = no pause)");
+
+/*
  * The SMU this ASIC implementation is driving, recorded once its tables are
  * up. Needed because the release below is requested from DC, which has no
  * route to a struct smu_context. A single pointer rather than a per-device
@@ -1074,6 +1087,7 @@
 {
 	struct smu_context *smu;
 	bool armed = false;
+	bool released = false;
 
 	if (!cs_od_unforce_ms)
 		return;
@@ -1098,6 +1112,7 @@
 					  CYAN_SKILLFISH_VDDC_MAGIC)) {
 			WRITE_ONCE(cs_od_force_suspended, true);
 			armed = true;
+			released = true;
 			if (cs_od_defer_debug)
 				dev_info(smu->adev->dev,
 					 "cs_od_defer: released forced GFX clock/voltage for link bring-up\n");
@@ -1105,6 +1120,10 @@
 	}
 	mutex_unlock(&cs_od_force_lock);
 
+	/* Outside the lock: this sleeps, and nothing else needs to wait on it. */
+	if (released && cs_od_unforce_settle_ms)
+		msleep(cs_od_unforce_settle_ms);
+
 	if (armed)
 		mod_delayed_work(system_wq, &cs_od_force_restore_work,
 				 msecs_to_jiffies(cs_od_unforce_ms));
